A||||9||fierogt88||supercharging the 2.8....||||1||
Z||000000||fierogt88||03-26-2001||01:56 AM||||If i could get my hands on a supercharger for the 3800, would there be any way to "down-size" it to work on a 2.8/3.1/3.4 with different sized pulleys?  Has anybody tried anything like this?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||03-26-2001||09:57 AM||||I had the same idea, but the guys a Magnuson gave me enough info to know that the Eaton M90 blower that comes on the 3800's is way too much blower for the engine. They recomend a M62, but nothing ever came from the factory with the m62 so you would have to buy a new one instead of junkyarding for one. There are companies that offer a supercharger kit, but it takes the spot occupied by the A/C compressor. Maybe when I get rich, I will buy the m62 kit.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierogt88||03-26-2001||12:02 PM||||So it's just too big for a 2.8, but what about a built up 3.4?||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||03-26-2001||12:42 PM||||What? 92-95 Buick 3800's had Eaton M-62. 94/95 had Telfon coated rotors. M-90 showed up in 96. <P>As for the M-90 being too much blower, while it is certainly capable of blowing too much air, you just put a bigger pulley on it and slow it down.<P>The bottom line is that any Eaton is large (45, 62, 90). Fitting one to a Fiero is a huge challenge. The 3.4L SC method of putting it off to the side is about the only viable option. Getting a belt to it is the next problem.||||reg||5
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||03-26-2001||06:50 PM||||Okay, GM put the Eaton off of a 3800 onto the Grand Am's 3.4. Along with whatever else GM did to the motor, (It was a concept car, they weren't going to leave everything else stock.) it put out 250hp.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||03-26-2001||08:26 PM||||Sorry for the mis-information. I was going on what the guys at Magnuson were telling me. You would think that they know what they were talking about. I had already decided to keep my M90 and just slow it down with some custom pulleys. To get the airflow I need, the m90 turns around 9500 RPMs but the m62 must spin almost 13000 for the same flow. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Terrybogin||03-27-2001||01:16 AM||||Greg was too embarassed to come back, but we did find this: <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=573975252" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=573975252</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||03-27-2001||01:55 AM||||Sorry Greg, I didn't mean to sledgehammer. <P>I believe the Eaton on the Grand Am 3.4L was an M-45. The 3800 was an M-62.<P>Mounting in on the side is an option, but we held an M-62 in there (a tad larger) and we couldn't see any practical location. Maybe down low might work with a long shaft.<P>Yes, for the same output the M-90 turns slower than the M-62 and slower than the M-45, but faster than the M-112.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>The numbers are the displacement of the blower in cubic inches.<P>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-27-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||pontiacs4me||03-27-2001||01:58 AM||||can you buy a supercharger say one like this <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=572102308&r=0&t=0" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=572102308&r=0&t=0</A>  and put it on a 1990 3800 out of a toronado?  What upgrades to the ecm will you have to do?  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||terryk||03-27-2001||02:19 PM||||I've been asked this question before and my default is answer is that if you want a superchargered Buick, buy one, don't try to convert. While it can be done, the cost and labor is terrible. You have to change all of the accessories mounting to accomodate the second drive belt. Tons of other stuff. New intake manifold, fuel rails, etc. <P>I'd consider a turbo before every thinking of trying to install the blower.||||reg||1
